    {"problem": "The second floor is a sauna while the AC runs flat out", "solution": "In a two-storey, cold air falls and the upstairs ducts sit at the end of the run. We check the charge and the coil first, then static pressure and the damper settings. Often the fix is airflow, not refrigerant."},
    {"problem": "Someone topped it up last year, and it is warm again", "solution": "That is an R-22 system with a leak. R-22 has been phased out in Canada and the remaining supply costs more every season, so a yearly top-up is the most expensive way to own an air conditioner. We pressure test to find the leak, then give you two numbers: the repair, and a replacement that runs on current refrigerant."},
    {"problem": "The air is cool but the house feels like a Port Credit boathouse", "solution": "An oversized or single-speed air conditioner satisfies the thermostat before it has run long enough to pull moisture from the air. We check the sizing against a heat-gain calculation and the blower speed setting. Sometimes a lower fan speed fixes it; sometimes the unit was simply too big from day one."},
    {"problem": "The outdoor unit clicks on, buzzes, and goes quiet", "solution": "A swollen run capacitor or a pitted contactor is the usual cause, and both are on the truck. If the compressor itself is pulling locked-rotor amps, we test it properly before giving you news you will not like."},
    {"problem": "Frost on the big copper line beside the furnace", "solution": "Ice means the coil is running too cold, from a filter that has not been changed since spring, a blower that is slowing down or a charge that has leaked away. We thaw it, find which one it was and fix that rather than just switching it back on."},
