  {"type": "split", "photo": "sys", "h2": "R-22 air conditioners in Mississauga: when to stop repairing",
   "html": "<p>If the data plate on your outdoor unit says R-22, the unit was built before 2010. In Mississauga that usually means it went in with a 1990s house in Meadowvale or Erin Mills, or replaced the original in a Cooksville bungalow. R-22 production and import have ended in Canada, so what is left is recycled stock at a rising price.</p>"
           "<p>Our rule is simple. A capacitor, contactor, fan motor or drain repair on an R-22 unit is fine; those parts do not care what refrigerant is inside. A leak repair plus a recharge, a compressor or an evaporator coil is where we stop and show you the replacement number, because you would be putting a large sum into refrigerant that is going away and a unit with no warranty left.</p>"
           "<p>The replacement we quote first is a cold-climate heat pump. It cools the same way an air conditioner does, runs on R-454B, and takes over most of your heating from the Enbridge furnace, which is what makes it eligible for the Home Renovation Savings Program. A straight central AC is still the right answer for some homes, and we quote that too. Our <a href='/ac/repair-maintenance/'>AC repair and maintenance page</a> covers the full list of faults we fix across Ontario, and <a href='/mississauga/'>our Mississauga HVAC page</a> covers the rest of the house.</p>"},
